  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/...\nIssued 313 AM MST THU NOV 19 2026\nCome late weekend, another strong upper-level low pressure system \nfrom off the upper Pacific coast will weaken as it moves onshore \ninto the northwest region. This weakening system will steer a \nshortwave trough across our area, returning precipitation chances \nearly Sunday. There remains good guidance agreement on the \nprecipitation coverage remaining highly limited and short-lived over \nthe more northwest portions of our area. Snow levels will hover \nbelow valley floors through most of Sunday. The Treasure Valley will \nsee around a 10-20% chance of a rain/snow mix in the early morning \nhours of Sunday. Mountains in the northern half of our CWA will see \na 40-60% chance of snow showers continuing through late Sunday. \nAnother weak embedded shortwave trough will dig across the upper \nGreat Basin through early Monday, bringing a 15-30% chance of light \nsnow showers over the easternmost portions of our CWA Monday \nmorning. Late Monday through Tuesday, an amplified ridge of high \npressure will build into the area and return drier conditions and a \nslight warming trend. Late Tuesday through Thursday, there is \nconsiderable guidance uncertainty over the trajectory of another \nstrong Pacific upper low moving into the region. It can either \nremain quasi-stationary off the coast of British Columbia and drive \nan eastward-moving swath of precipitation over our area from Tuesday \nthrough Thursday, or dig directly south of our area into the Great \nBasin and bring very limited precipitation chances through the\nweek. For now, the long-term guidance projects next Wednesday \nthrough Thursday to remain unsettled with moderate to high \nprecipitation chances returning areawide.",
